coder v0.0.0-devel

USAGE:
  coder run [flags] --template <template> [-- command...]

  Run a command in a workspace of its own, with an identity of its own

  Run a command as a workload. The workload gets its own identity, sponsored by
  you. It can do nothing in Coder unless you give it scopes, and it never
  receives your credentials. The command's output is streamed and its exit code
  returned.
  
  The workload gets a workspace of its own, which its identity owns and shares
  with you. The workspace is made from the template for this one command,
  claiming one that was built ahead of time when the template keeps such
  workspaces, and deleted when the command is over.
    - Run a command and see who it is:
  
       $ coder run --template agents -- coder identity
  
    - Let a workload read templates, and nothing else:
  
       $ coder run --template agents --scope template:read -- coder templates
  list
  
    - Keep the workspace afterwards, to look around in it:
  
       $ coder run --template agents --keep --name fix-1 -- make test
  
    - Work in a workload's workspace with a terminal:
  
       $ coder run --template agents -it -- sh
  
    - List your running workloads:
  
       $ coder run --list
  
    - List all of them, with what became of each:
  
       $ coder run --all
  
    - Remove a workload, and its workspace with it:
  
       $ coder run --remove fix-1

OPTIONS:
  -a, --all bool
          List your workloads, including the most recent ones that are over.

  -c, --column [name|workspace|status|sponsor|scopes|command|identity|sandbox|started|finished] (default: name,workspace,status,sponsor,scopes,command,started)
          Columns to display in table output.

  -d, --detach bool
          Start the workload and print its name instead of following its output.

  -i, --interactive bool
          Keep the command's input open. Use it with --tty, as -it.

      --keep bool
          Keep the workspace when the command is over. It stays as it is, and
          you can enter it. Removing the workload removes it.

  -l, --list bool
          List your running workloads instead of running one.

      --logs string
          Print the output of a workload, by name or identity, instead of
          running one. A running workload is followed until it ends. For one
          that is over, the end of its output was kept.

      --name string
          A name for the workload. Its workspace is named like it.

  -o, --output table|json (default: table)
          Output format.

      --preset string
          The preset of the template to use. A workspace that was built ahead of
          time for the preset is claimed when there is one.

      --remove string
          Remove the named workload, and its workspace, instead of running one.

      --scope string-array
          A permission the workload may use, as an API token scope such as
          workspace:read. Repeat it for several. Without any, the workload can
          do nothing in Coder.

      --template string
          The template the workload's workspace is made from. The workload's
          identity owns the workspace and shares it with you.

  -t, --tty bool
          Give the command a terminal and attach to it. Use it with
          --interactive, as -it.

———
Run `coder --help` for a list of global options.
